            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Start with the Funnel Stage, Not the Channel</h2>
<h3>1. Should the budget be divided by channel or conversion stage?</h3>
<p><strong>Divide it by conversion stage first, then assign channels.</strong></p>
<p>A practical starting point is to reserve 10%–15% for creative, landing-page, and tracking tests; allocate 60%–70% to acquisition; keep 15%–20% for remarketing; and retain around 10% as flexible funds. New stores should avoid depending on a single platform.</p>
<p>Use the target customer acquisition cost to work backward:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Monthly order target ÷ target conversion rate × acceptable CAC</p>
</blockquote>
<p>If the target CAC exceeds what the business can sustain, adding more budget will only scale the loss.</p>
<h3>2. How should budget be distributed across Meta, Google, TikTok, and email?\n</h3>
<p>There is no universal split. Match each channel to its role:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Meta:</strong> Rapid validation for visually driven products.</li>
<li><strong>Google:</strong> Capture high-intent category, brand, and product searches.</li>
<li><strong>TikTok:</strong> Test new products and creator-led creative, with enough budget to allow learning.</li>
<li><strong>Email and remarketing:</strong> Protect existing demand and recover abandoned checkouts with relatively limited spend.</li>
</ul>
<p>An anonymized utility app initially placed 60% of its budget on Meta. After reviewing order quality, it shifted to 40% Meta, 35% Google, 15% remarketing, and 10% testing. The key was aligning spending with the actual order mix.</p>
<h2>Account Funding and Budget Control</h2>
<h3>3. Should the budget change daily or follow predefined rules?\n</h3>
<p><strong>Use predefined thresholds instead of reacting emotionally.</strong></p>
<p>Track budget, spend, CPA, conversion rate, and balance in a simple dashboard. If a campaign spends 1.5 times the target CPA without producing an order, pause it or replace the creative. If CPA improves for three consecutive days and lead quality remains stable, increase the budget by 10%–20%.</p>
<h3>4. How should multi-account teams handle funding and balance alerts?\n</h3>
<p>Do not wait until an account is nearly out of funds. Set balance alerts at both 20% and 30%, and keep at least one backup account ready. Use weekly rolling deposits rather than placing an entire quarterly budget into one account.</p>
<p>A shared tracker should record the account, currency, balance, daily budget, and owner. Before scaling, verify that the payment method and billing entity are stable. Frequent changes to payment details can trigger account reviews or restrictions.</p>
<h2>Reconciliation and Scaling: Which Signals Justify More Spend?</h2>
<h3>5. If the ad platform reports orders, is the campaign profitable?</h3>
<p><strong>Not necessarily.</strong></p>
<p>Deduct refunds, chargebacks, platform fees, fulfillment costs, and discounts, then reconcile purchases within a consistent attribution window. One cross-border e-commerce team found that Meta appeared to generate strong ROI, but brand organic orders had been counted as paid conversions. After aligning UTMs, promo codes, and campaign-level data, only two campaigns were genuinely ready to scale.</p>
<p>Before increasing spend, confirm three conditions: the target CPA is not deteriorating, refund and chargeback rates remain acceptable, and inventory, landing pages, and customer support can handle additional demand. Raising daily spend from $1,000 to $1,500 is safer than doubling it overnight. If traffic quality deteriorates or suspicious orders increase, roll back immediately.</p>
<h3>Final Recommendation</h3>
<p>Effective budget control depends on clear stop-loss, testing, and scaling rules. Review allocation weekly and reconcile ad-account funding, platform spend, attributed revenue, and actual payments monthly. When funding, account management, and finance reconciliation stay aligned, multi-account budgets become much easier to control.</p>
